ved and  made it through all the tough times van  newkirk is the interim president of the  historically black  fisk university in nashville tennessee  founded  in 1866. we turn out folks like w.b du  bois ida wales  john hope franklin john lewis we have a  history of  turning out folks who are endued and  with social justice about  one-third of hbcus have experienced  record increases in applications  and enrollment over the past three years  according to the center for minority  serving institutions  why do you think we're seeing  record-breaking enrollment  when we look at what's going on in the  nation right now issues around social  justice  and racism  [Applause]  i see students coming back to hbcus  coming home  want to make sure that we move more into  the mainstream and we become a  university that  is sustainable that sustainability  becomes even more challenging with  students in need  according to the american council on  education  over 70 percent of hbcu students have  limited finances  why have so many of the hbcus  struggled financially well for two  reasons one  uh the black community is one of the  poorest communities in america  and and frankly because philanthropy  michael lomax of the united negro  college fund says  hbcus have rarely gotten large donations  resulting in endowments that are about  70 percent smaller than  other schools but 2020 ushered in the  biggest donations in  hbcu history 120 million from netflix  ceo  reed hastings and half a billion from  mackenzie scott  the ex-wife of jeff bezos these big  gifts mean  that american philanthropy is beginning  to see  that equity and equality mean equitable  giving  as well students tell me you can't put a  price tag on what they gain  at hbcus i feel like i'm  getting a foundation to help  develop the framework of how i'm going  to live the rest of my life  fears gave me confidence i could be  myself in  every arena you know and i didn't have  to change who i was  for cbs this morning hbcu grad  and howard university alum michelle  miller  michelle listen we are we are a better  country because  hbcus exist yeah i mean to hear that  stat there 80  of the nation's black judges 50 of its  black  doctors but then you look at the  endowments that's huge right that we  you know you don't have the same support  financially and that's why that money  needs to keep flowing to those

